WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.645
BatchNorm backward, and what the formula says.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:07.085
Exercise 3 derives BatchNorm’s backward pass as one expression.

3
00:00:07.365 --> 00:00:19.099
The derivation is a good workout: x_i affects the output through three paths, directly through x hat i, through the mean (which every x hat depends on), and through the variance (likewise).

4
00:00:19.379 --> 00:00:24.989
Summing the three, as the chain rule demands, and simplifying gives the compact formula above.

5
00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:30.773
Ioffe and Szegedy’s paper states the same chain-rule decomposition in its section 3.

6
00:00:31.053 --> 00:00:33.123
Read the formula as a projection.

7
00:00:33.403 --> 00:00:47.397
Inside the parentheses, n dy_i minus the sum of dy removes the component of the gradient that would shift all examples equally, because BatchNorm’s output is invariant to such a shift (the mean is subtracted).

8
00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:59.709
Then minus x hat i times the sum of dy x hat removes the component along x hat, because the output is also invariant to rescaling all inputs (the variance divides it out).

9
00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:06.432
The gradient that reaches x is the upstream gradient with exactly those two directions projected away.

10
00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:17.208
That’s a nice example of the math explaining the behavior: BatchNorm makes a layer insensitive to the scale of its inputs, so gradients can’t push along those directions.

11
00:01:17.488 --> 00:01:22.693
The Bessel digression at 01:05:17 matters for the details.

12
00:01:22.973 --> 00:01:26.835
The unbiased variance estimator divides by n minus 1.

13
00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:41.621
PyTorch’s BatchNorm1d normalizes with the biased version (divide by n) during training but tracks the unbiased version for the running variance used at inference, so training and inference use slightly different statistics.

14
00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:50.669
The lecture argues for using the unbiased estimate consistently, and its formula carries the corresponding n over (n minus 1) factor.

15
00:01:50.949 --> 00:01:54.256
For a batch of 32, the difference is about 3 percent.
